Don't hold it like that. Hold the tail.

1791
01:12:02,289 --> 01:12:03,789
- The tail?
- Yes.

1792
01:12:04,659 --> 01:12:05,699
Yes, hold it tight.

1793
01:12:05,829 --> 01:12:06,999
That's it. Wait.

1794
01:12:08,199 --> 01:12:09,199
That's it.

1795
01:12:10,429 --> 01:12:11,699
(Yoon Seo succeeded.)

1796
01:12:12,539 --> 01:12:13,839
Gosh, it's huge.

1797
01:12:14,099 --> 01:12:17,109
- We caught a lot of big fish.
- The captain said we'd find...

1798
01:12:17,109 --> 01:12:19,339
bigger Japanese Spanish mackerel at this spot.

1799
01:12:19,339 --> 01:12:20,379
He's a pro.

1800
01:12:21,549 --> 01:12:23,849
(You Seok scoops up the fish with a net.)

1801
01:12:24,819 --> 01:12:27,379
(The huge Japanese Spanish mackerel...)

1802
01:12:27,949 --> 01:12:29,849
(weighs down the scoop net.)

1803
01:12:30,749 --> 01:12:32,359
- What is that?
- A Japanese Spanish mackerel.

1804
01:12:32,859 --> 01:12:33,919
The one you caught earlier.

1805
01:12:34,959 --> 01:12:37,189
(Yoon Seo holds it tight...)

1806
01:12:40,099 --> 01:12:42,969
(and successfully transfers it.)

1807
01:12:44,169 --> 01:12:45,499
(A net full of fish)

1808
01:12:45,869 --> 01:12:46,899
My gosh.

1809
01:12:48,269 --> 01:12:49,669
(Here he goes again.)

1810
01:12:50,569 --> 01:12:51,909
- Oh, my.
- Oh, gosh.

1811
01:12:53,079 --> 01:12:54,109
What are they?

1812
01:12:55,279 --> 01:12:57,449
(Here's some work for you!)

1813
01:12:58,749 --> 01:12:59,819
(Sorting them right away)

1814
01:12:59,819 --> 01:13:01,649
(Skilled)

1815
01:13:02,649 --> 01:13:03,689
I've gotten the hang of it.

1816
01:13:04,719 --> 01:13:05,989
(Walking over)

1817
01:13:06,989 --> 01:13:08,059
(Japanese Spanish mackerel)

1818
01:13:10,089 --> 01:13:11,099
(Japanese Spanish mackerel)

1819
01:13:11,099 --> 01:13:12,699
Look at Yoon Seo go.

1820
01:13:12,699 --> 01:13:14,569
(Carrying two shark-like Japanese Spanish mackerel)

1821
01:13:15,469 --> 01:13:17,799
(The scoop-net duo)

1822
01:13:17,899 --> 01:13:18,899
(A net full of fish)

1823
01:13:20,039 --> 01:13:21,839
- Can you scoop it up?
- Yes.

1824
01:13:22,739 --> 01:13:24,069
You've got a good scoopful.

1825
01:13:25,079 --> 01:13:26,939
(Here goes Yoon Seo first.)

1826
01:13:27,579 --> 01:13:29,849
(Jung Ah scoops up some fish right after Yoon Seo.)

1827
01:13:31,149 --> 01:13:32,619
- What are they?
- Those are mackerel.

1828
01:13:32,619 --> 01:13:33,679
Mackerel?

1829
01:13:33,679 --> 01:13:34,719
(The mackerel are thrashing around in the net.)

1830
01:13:34,919 --> 01:13:38,719
(Mackerel, In season: July to October)

1831
01:13:39,219 --> 01:13:40,559
Mackerel.

1832
01:13:40,989 --> 01:13:42,529
- My gosh.
- It's a staple fish.

1833
01:13:43,489 --> 01:13:44,529
It's a staple fish.

1834
01:13:44,529 --> 01:13:45,559
(Surprised)

1835
01:13:45,799 --> 01:13:47,229
(Scared)

1836
01:13:48,169 --> 01:13:50,199
- The fish is in vibrate mode.
- What's up with it?

1837
01:13:50,199 --> 01:13:51,239
It's impossible to catch.

1838
01:13:51,869 --> 01:13:53,239
It's so fresh and so funny.

1839
01:13:53,239 --> 01:13:54,299
(Someone's calling.)

1840
01:13:54,809 --> 01:13:56,269
Calm down.

1841
01:13:59,139 --> 01:14:00,779
(Yoon Seo answers the call.)

1842
01:14:05,749 --> 01:14:06,779
What's with that one?

1843
01:14:06,779 --> 01:14:07,789
(Sun Young is also getting a call.)

1844
01:14:08,889 --> 01:14:09,989
(A group of fish)

1845
01:14:10,249 --> 01:14:12,259
(Calls come flooding in now that word is out the siblings are here.)

1846
01:14:13,059 --> 01:14:15,959
No. Calm down, sir.

1847
01:14:15,959 --> 01:14:17,259
(Flustered)

1848
01:14:17,259 --> 01:14:18,299
What?

1849
01:14:19,959 --> 01:14:22,429
(The set-net newbie is calming down the excited fish.)

1850
01:14:23,129 --> 01:14:25,799
Grab the fish by the head, and it'll calm...

1851
01:14:27,239 --> 01:14:28,309
Calm down.

1852
01:14:29,669 --> 01:14:31,109
It's impossible to catch.

1853
01:14:31,739 --> 01:14:33,979
(Pick up the phone!)

1854
01:14:34,449 --> 01:14:35,449
Okay.

1855
01:14:36,279 --> 01:14:38,849
(While the youngest is struggling to answer the calls)

1856
01:14:39,849 --> 01:14:41,689
- I'm going to catch you.
- I caught them!

1857
01:14:41,689 --> 01:14:43,049
I caught some mackerel.

1858
01:14:44,049 --> 01:14:45,089
Come here.

1859
01:14:46,819 --> 01:14:47,859
It's heavy.

1860
01:14:47,859 --> 01:14:50,589
(The siblings are scooping up nets full of mackerel.)

1861
01:14:51,399 --> 01:14:52,399
(Emptying)

1862
01:14:52,399 --> 01:14:54,599
Jung Ah caught a bunch of mackerel.

1863
01:14:55,829 --> 01:14:58,739
Gosh, look at this one. A mackerel.

1864
01:14:59,599 --> 01:15:00,669
It's really strong.

1865
01:15:00,669 --> 01:15:01,709
(Groaning)

1866
01:15:02,869 --> 01:15:03,869
Okay.

1867
01:15:04,469 --> 01:15:07,179
There's a trick to this. Like so.

1868
01:15:07,609 --> 01:15:10,049
That's it. Right at that spot.

1869
01:15:10,049 --> 01:15:11,819
(Sun Young has gotten used to using the net.)

1870
01:15:12,679 --> 01:15:14,419
(After scooping up fish with the net with ease)

1871
01:15:15,089 --> 01:15:16,989
(Sorting the fish out)

1872
01:15:19,562 --> 01:15:21,302
(Sorting the fish out)

1873
01:15:26,864 --> 01:15:27,894
(Groaning)

1874
01:15:27,894 --> 01:15:29,264
(Don't let your guard down.)

1875
01:15:29,264 --> 01:15:30,464
That really surprised me.

1876
01:15:30,935 --> 01:15:32,005
What's with it?

1877
01:15:34,005 --> 01:15:36,375
That guy. Is it a bird?

1878
01:15:36,987 --> 01:15:38,397
(It's a large silver pomfret.)

1879
01:15:38,527 --> 01:15:40,857
I'd better wait until it gets tired.

1880
01:15:41,097 --> 01:15:42,227
I can't.

1881
01:15:42,927 --> 01:15:45,197
All right, we're almost done here.

1882
01:15:45,197 --> 01:15:48,137
(2 hours into the set net fishing labor)

1883
01:15:56,347 --> 01:15:59,717
This is no time to be messing around. It's a war every single day.

1884
01:16:02,647 --> 01:16:04,517
Mackerel. That's it.

1885
01:16:05,217 --> 01:16:07,457
I'll do my best for our viewers.

1886
01:16:12,727 --> 01:16:14,327
Is it hot? I'll just let myself sweat.

1887
01:16:14,327 --> 01:16:15,927
I can take a shower afterward.

1888
01:16:22,467 --> 01:16:25,907
I'm the type of person who gets energized by working.

1889
01:16:27,377 --> 01:16:28,377
Let's do this.

1890
01:16:28,377 --> 01:16:29,877
My gosh, look at this one.

1891
01:16:30,347 --> 01:16:31,477
Hold on.

1892
01:16:32,077 --> 01:16:33,577
I'm no longer seasick.

1893
01:16:33,577 --> 01:16:36,847
(Past those first moments of excitement and uncertainty)

1894
01:16:40,057 --> 01:16:42,557
You're good at it. I'm sure you'll get paid today.

1895
01:16:42,557 --> 01:16:43,687
(Laughing)

1896
01:16:45,157 --> 01:16:47,727
(Through all the sweat they've shed together)

1897
01:16:47,727 --> 01:16:49,397
- It's a whale.
- A whale.

1898
01:16:49,727 --> 01:16:51,667
- This one is huge.
- It's gigantic.

1899
01:16:52,597 --> 01:16:54,137
Let's push this away.

1900
01:16:55,407 --> 01:16:58,907
(Out on the summer sea in sweltering Yeosu)

1901
01:17:02,907 --> 01:17:05,317
(The siblings reflect once again on what inspired them to begin.)

1902
01:17:08,487 --> 01:17:09,517
We're done.

1903
01:17:09,717 --> 01:17:11,787
(The set net fishing labor is over.)

1904
01:17:12,357 --> 01:17:13,817
(The captain brings something over.)

1905
01:17:13,817 --> 01:17:15,457
- My gosh.
- You all did a great job.

1906
01:17:15,457 --> 01:17:17,227
I cut up some sashimi on the boat.

1907
01:17:17,387 --> 01:17:18,897
- Try it.
- Thank you.

1908
01:17:18,897 --> 01:17:20,797
- What kind of fish is it?
- Silver pomfret.

1909
01:17:20,797 --> 01:17:21,927
- It's in season.
- Silver pomfret sashimi?

1910
01:17:22,297 --> 01:17:23,327
(Silver pomfret at the peak of its season)

1911
01:17:23,327 --> 01:17:24,597
- Thank you.
- Is this chojang?

1912
01:17:25,497 --> 01:17:26,597
It's Korean pomfret.

1913
01:17:26,597 --> 01:17:28,707
Korean pomfret tastes better with the bones left in.

1914
01:17:28,707 --> 01:17:29,837
(Freshly prepared right on the boat)

1915
01:17:31,507 --> 01:17:32,607
Thank you.

1916
01:17:33,607 --> 01:17:35,907
(A piece of silver pomfret dipped in chojang)

1917
01:17:36,847 --> 01:17:38,217
It's a rare treat, right?

1918
01:17:38,217 --> 01:17:39,777
- Yes.
- You can't have it every day.

1919
01:17:39,777 --> 01:17:41,687
Korean pomfret can only be enjoyed during summer.

1920
01:17:41,687 --> 01:17:43,387
- It's the best during summer.
- Right.

1921
01:17:44,547 --> 01:17:48,087
(Yoon Seo also tries a piece of seasonal silver pomfret.)

1922
01:17:48,357 --> 01:17:49,687
- It's savory.
- Isn't it good?

1923
01:17:50,927 --> 01:17:51,927
- How is it?
- It's savory.

1924
01:17:51,927 --> 01:17:52,927
- It's savory.
- It's good.

1925
01:17:52,927 --> 01:17:53,957
- Is it?
- Yes.

1926
01:17:54,497 --> 01:17:56,767
The bones aren't that tough.

1927
01:17:56,767 --> 01:17:58,267
(After hearing it's good, Sun Young tries a piece too.)

1928
01:17:58,267 --> 01:17:59,767
(Her eyes widen.)

1929
01:17:59,767 --> 01:18:01,807
I can tell it's silver pomfret the moment I taste it.

1930
01:18:01,807 --> 01:18:04,407
- Amazing.
- Can you tell just by trying it?

1931
01:18:04,707 --> 01:18:06,107
I know the moment I taste it.

1932
01:18:06,107 --> 01:18:08,147
Sun Young, aren't you from the US?

1933
01:18:09,007 --> 01:18:11,077
Americans don't eat sashimi.

1934
01:18:11,077 --> 01:18:12,847
- Exactly.
- What is it called in the US?

1935
01:18:12,847 --> 01:18:13,947
How did you know it was silver pomfret?

1936
01:18:13,947 --> 01:18:15,147
Silver pomfret in the US?

1937
01:18:15,387 --> 01:18:17,417
- Bottle fish.
- Fish.

1938
01:18:17,417 --> 01:18:18,557
- Bottle fish.
- Yes.

1939
01:18:18,557 --> 01:18:20,157
- Bottle fish.
- Yes, bottle fish.

1940
01:18:20,157 --> 01:18:21,357
- What?
- Bottle fish.

1941
01:18:21,357 --> 01:18:22,627
(Culture shock)

1942
01:18:22,627 --> 01:18:23,727
Oh, my gosh.

1943
01:18:23,827 --> 01:18:25,457
(Laughing)

1944
01:18:27,197 --> 01:18:29,197
I'm so happy I'm not seasick anymore.

1945
01:18:29,197 --> 01:18:31,867
(As they chat and enjoy sashimi on board)

1946
01:18:35,067 --> 01:18:36,137
We're back.

1947
01:18:36,137 --> 01:18:37,137
(They're already pulling into the dock.)

1948
01:18:37,137 --> 01:18:38,737
- We're home.
- We're back.

1949
01:18:39,137 --> 01:18:40,907
- Bye.
- Bye.

1950
01:18:41,677 --> 01:18:44,607
- Dear viewers, we're back.
- Hi.

1951
01:18:44,777 --> 01:18:47,547
Viewers. We're back.

1952
01:18:48,047 --> 01:18:49,417
- We're fine.
- Sun Young.

1953
01:18:49,417 --> 01:18:51,447
- We're not one bit tired.
- We have to go now.

1954
01:18:51,447 --> 01:18:53,717
- Viewers. We're not tired, okay?
- We need to go.

1955
01:18:55,857 --> 01:18:57,157
We're fine.

1956
01:18:58,697 --> 01:19:00,127
Great work in this heat.

1957
01:19:00,127 --> 01:19:01,357
- It's not much.
- Thank you.

1958
01:19:01,357 --> 01:19:02,767
Cook them up and enjoy them.

1959
01:19:02,767 --> 01:19:04,067
- Okay, thank you.
- Thank you.

1960
01:19:05,137 --> 01:19:07,167
(From wild octopus...)

1961
01:19:09,437 --> 01:19:12,707
(to large silver pomfret in season)

1962
01:19:12,777 --> 01:19:13,807
- Thank you.
- No problem.

1963
01:19:13,807 --> 01:19:14,977
We'll enjoy them.

1964
01:19:14,977 --> 01:19:16,677
- Thank you so much.
- Great work.

1965
01:19:16,677 --> 01:19:17,807
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1966
01:19:21,377 --> 01:19:24,647
(The various seafood that the siblings caught today)

1967
01:19:25,957 --> 01:19:29,687
(Brimming with freshness straight from the sea)

1968
01:19:33,227 --> 01:19:37,427
(Sets off on a long journey to destinations across the country)

1969
01:19:37,827 --> 01:19:39,197
It's only an 11-minute ride.

1970
01:19:39,197 --> 01:19:40,337
(The siblings return to town.)

1971
01:19:41,097 --> 01:19:42,107
(Glancing)

1972
01:19:42,107 --> 01:19:43,267
The mural is really on my mind.

1973
01:19:43,937 --> 01:19:45,777
I can't help but look at it when we come here.

1974
01:19:45,777 --> 01:19:46,937
The mural is all I can see.

1975
01:19:48,547 --> 01:19:49,947
(A welcome sight)

1976
01:19:52,717 --> 01:19:54,477
- It's still there.
- How pretty.

1977
01:19:54,477 --> 01:19:55,487
I keep checking it out.

1978
01:19:56,417 --> 01:19:58,357
I still have to paint the stingray's eyes.

1979
01:19:58,657 --> 01:20:00,287
- The stingray's eyes...
- The stingray's eyes.

1980
01:20:00,287 --> 01:20:01,627
and the turtle's eyes.

1981
01:20:01,887 --> 01:20:03,587
- I...
- The stingray needs its eyes...

1982
01:20:03,587 --> 01:20:07,327
and the stripes down its fin.

1983
01:20:08,157 --> 01:20:10,267
Let's wash up quickly and go work on the mural.

1984
01:20:11,667 --> 01:20:13,197
We'll do as told.

1985
01:20:13,267 --> 01:20:14,697
Just give us the instructions.

1986
01:20:14,697 --> 01:20:15,707
(They need to prep dinner and finish the mural.)

1987
01:20:15,707 --> 01:20:17,637
- No, it was fun.
- Let's do this, Yoon Seo.

1988
01:20:17,637 --> 01:20:18,637
Okay.

1989
01:20:18,977 --> 01:20:22,777
Yoon Seo, what color should...

1990
01:20:22,777 --> 01:20:24,547
- the stingray's stripe be?
- Purple.

1991
01:20:24,547 --> 01:20:25,647
A deeper purple than that one.

1992
01:20:25,947 --> 01:20:27,847
Tell me which parts to color in.

1993
01:20:27,847 --> 01:20:30,417
We're done coloring everything in.

1994
01:20:30,417 --> 01:20:32,657
I'm not good at adding in the details.

1995
01:20:32,657 --> 01:20:33,887
- So...
- Yes?

1996
01:20:33,887 --> 01:20:35,027
(Mural to-do list)

1997
01:20:35,027 --> 01:20:36,687
( Net, turtle's eyes, background touch-ups, stingray details)

1998
01:20:36,687 --> 01:20:39,057
(Lots of detail work left)

1999
01:20:39,097 --> 01:20:41,797
Okay. Blue and white.

2000
01:20:41,797 --> 01:20:43,667
(Time to put the finishing touches on the mural!)

2001
01:20:44,267 --> 01:20:46,137
Is this how I should do it, Ms. Roh?

2002
01:20:46,137 --> 01:20:47,167
Yes.

2003
01:20:47,767 --> 01:20:50,307
Hey, turtle. Be happy.

2004
01:20:51,067 --> 01:20:53,907
I'm painting the turtle's eyes, the most important part.

2005
01:20:55,007 --> 01:20:56,307
Yes, the eyes are the most important.

2006
01:20:56,307 --> 01:20:57,877
Emotions are all in the eyes.

2007
01:20:58,847 --> 01:21:01,117
(Leaning close to the wall, working carefully)

2008
01:21:01,117 --> 01:21:03,147
Viewers, my hands are shaking.

2009
01:21:05,857 --> 01:21:09,827
(Putting their hearts into every stroke)

2010
01:21:10,657 --> 01:21:15,127
(Carefully tending to every nook and cranny)

2011
01:21:15,767 --> 01:21:17,097
It's coming together.

2012
01:21:17,097 --> 01:21:19,937
(The siblings fill in every last bit of the mural.)

2013
01:21:22,707 --> 01:21:25,377
(After working away for a while)

2014
01:21:25,377 --> 01:21:26,407
Oh, my.

2015
01:21:26,407 --> 01:21:27,907
(Holding her lower back)

2016
01:21:28,507 --> 01:21:30,377
(Current time, 6pm)

2017
01:21:30,377 --> 01:21:32,947
Let's go make dinner after we paint a bit more.

2018
01:21:33,117 --> 01:21:34,647
- I'd better get going.
- Let's go.

2019
01:21:35,217 --> 01:21:36,217
Yoon Seo.

2020
01:21:36,417 --> 01:21:37,947
- I'm leaving first.
- Okay.

2021
01:21:37,947 --> 01:21:39,557
We'll go make dinner.

2022
01:21:39,557 --> 01:21:40,587
- Okay.
- Okay.

2023
01:21:40,587 --> 01:21:43,127
Wrap it up, okay? We're off to make dinner.

2024
01:21:43,127 --> 01:21:44,227
Okay.

2025
01:21:44,227 --> 01:21:45,757
(Leaving the mural to the two, they head home first!)

2026
01:21:46,057 --> 01:21:48,427
I'm going to finish up the ray.

2027
01:21:48,427 --> 01:21:50,297
Okay, stingray!

2028
01:21:50,367 --> 01:21:51,597
Okay, stingray!

2029
01:21:51,597 --> 01:21:54,737
(The youngest two chant to keep their spirits up.)

2030
01:21:54,737 --> 01:21:57,207
This is the biggest piece I've ever worked on.

2031
01:21:59,277 --> 01:22:01,107
This is my very first piece, Ms. Roh.

2032
01:22:01,107 --> 01:22:03,307
(Addressing each other formally while working on the mural)

2033
01:22:03,307 --> 01:22:06,947
Art was one area I realized I had...

2034
01:22:06,947 --> 01:22:08,017
- no talent in.
- How come?

2035
01:22:08,017 --> 01:22:09,117
Even more so than music.

2036
01:22:09,117 --> 01:22:11,447
How come? You're good at drawing.

2037
01:22:11,717 --> 01:22:12,887
The turtle, for example.

2038
01:22:12,887 --> 01:22:14,657
The turtle turned out cute, even in my opinion.

2039
01:22:15,387 --> 01:22:16,557
Maybe because it's my baby.

2040
01:22:16,557 --> 01:22:17,587
(Daddy?)

2041
01:22:18,497 --> 01:22:19,497
(Yoon Seo is put off.)

2042
01:22:19,497 --> 01:22:20,697
I'm very happy with it.

2043
01:22:21,157 --> 01:22:22,667
I no longer want to praise him.

2044
01:22:23,727 --> 01:22:25,137
I want to take back my compliment.

2045
01:22:25,367 --> 01:22:26,637
When you go to a kids' cafe...

2046
01:22:26,637 --> 01:22:28,067
- Kids' cafe?
- Yes.

2047
01:22:28,067 --> 01:22:29,507
You only have eyes for your own kid.

2048
01:22:29,737 --> 01:22:30,837
I feel the same about it.

2049
01:22:30,837 --> 01:22:31,877
(You Seok brought it to life with his brush.)

2050
01:22:31,877 --> 01:22:33,037
Really, they're all pretty.

2051
01:22:33,177 --> 01:22:34,637
But only mine stands out to me.

2052
01:22:35,077 --> 01:22:36,277
My baby that I drew.

2053
01:22:37,577 --> 01:22:40,517
(Meanwhile, Artist Roh is laser-focused.)

2054
01:22:41,147 --> 01:22:42,247
It's cute.

2055
01:22:42,247 --> 01:22:43,547
(Cute)

2056
01:22:43,687 --> 01:22:44,687
It's cute.

2057
01:22:44,687 --> 01:22:45,987
(Daddy Turtle watches on.)

2058
01:22:45,987 --> 01:22:47,787
Paint my turtle's eyes for me.

2059
01:22:48,157 --> 01:22:49,257
- Your turtle?
- Yes.

2060
01:22:50,027 --> 01:22:51,827
The eyes look dead.

2061
01:22:51,827 --> 01:22:52,927
(A turtle makeover request?)

2062
01:22:52,927 --> 01:22:54,227
(Let's put some life into those eyes!)

2063
01:22:54,227 --> 01:22:56,097
Let's see.

2064
01:22:56,097 --> 01:22:57,227
It's looking cute now.

2065
01:22:57,227 --> 01:22:58,397
Its eyes are a little too small.

2066
01:22:58,397 --> 01:22:59,397
(A sharp look)

2067
01:23:00,337 --> 01:23:01,597
The eyes are bigger than yours.

2068
01:23:02,067 --> 01:23:03,307
I meant the pupils.

2069
01:23:03,567 --> 01:23:05,877
- They're hard to make out.
- It's the glow.

2070
01:23:05,877 --> 01:23:07,937
The black circle is its pupil, You Seok.

2071
01:23:07,937 --> 01:23:10,047
Turtles have black pupils.

2072
01:23:10,047 --> 01:23:11,447
- Is that so?
- The glow.

2073
01:23:11,677 --> 01:23:12,717
Okay.

2074
01:23:12,717 --> 01:23:13,777
Seriously.

2075
01:23:14,177 --> 01:23:15,317
Turtle.

2076
01:23:15,317 --> 01:23:16,317
(Despite it all,)

2077
01:23:16,317 --> 01:23:17,317
My turtle is cute.

2078
01:23:17,317 --> 01:23:19,617
(the turtle is still cute.)

2079
01:23:20,287 --> 01:23:22,727
Okay, all that's left is the neck.

2080
01:23:22,727 --> 01:23:24,087
(Let's hurry, You Seok! There's not much left!)

2081
01:23:24,287 --> 01:23:25,857
Let's erase such parts.

2082
01:23:25,857 --> 01:23:27,457
(Filling in the empty background)

2083
01:23:28,227 --> 01:23:31,397
(Painting over the smears to make it look neat)

2084
01:23:31,397 --> 01:23:33,397
The color of the neck and face is a bit...

2085
01:23:33,397 --> 01:23:34,737
(Each focuses on their own section.)

2086
01:23:34,997 --> 01:23:37,467
It's so that you can tell the neck apart from the face.

2087
01:23:37,467 --> 01:23:38,607
(Once she distinguishes the sailor's neck from his face)

2088
01:23:38,607 --> 01:23:41,947
I think we can finish it off.

2089
01:23:41,947 --> 01:23:43,077
(Mural to-do list: 3. Background touch-ups)

2090
01:23:43,077 --> 01:23:44,477
- We're done.
- Right?

2091
01:23:44,477 --> 01:23:46,277
We only have to touch up the background.

2092
01:23:46,277 --> 01:23:47,747
(They're almost done with the list!)

2093
01:23:48,687 --> 01:23:52,117
(The sun that blazed overhead now casts long shadows.)

2094
01:23:52,417 --> 01:23:54,417
(What are the neighbors in Seongdu Village preparing for dinner?)

2095
01:23:54,717 --> 01:23:55,927
(Guarded by the fierce Ddol)

2096
01:23:55,927 --> 01:23:57,127
(Standing proud)

2097
01:23:57,757 --> 01:24:00,197
(Jeong Oe Sim's house)

2098
01:24:00,197 --> 01:24:01,927
I'm making Japanese Spanish mackerel jeon.

2099
01:24:01,927 --> 01:24:04,797
It's Japanese Spanish mackerel from Namhae in Yeosu.

2100
01:24:04,897 --> 01:24:06,037
The fish was caught today.

2101
01:24:06,797 --> 01:24:08,807
I thinly slice the flesh so it's tender.

2102
01:24:08,807 --> 01:24:11,067
(Thinly slicing the flesh for a tender bite)

2103
01:24:11,067 --> 01:24:12,877
It won't taste good if it's bland.

2104
01:24:13,207 --> 01:24:14,807
Pan-frying powder works best.

2105
01:24:18,247 --> 01:24:21,117
(Coat it in beaten egg and pan-fry it.)

2106
01:24:21,817 --> 01:24:23,247
It doesn't taste fishy.

2107
01:24:23,247 --> 01:24:24,687
(Without a hint of fishiness)

2108
01:24:24,687 --> 01:24:26,657
It's tender and tastes clean.

2109
01:24:26,657 --> 01:24:28,857
(Tender Japanese Spanish mackerel jeon with a clean flavor)

2110
01:24:28,857 --> 01:24:31,587
The bitter lettuce tastes great because it's from our own field.

2111
01:24:31,587 --> 01:24:35,097
It's crunchy and slightly bitter.

2112
01:24:35,097 --> 01:24:36,627
(Bitter lettuce kimchi with an addictively bitter bite)

2113
01:24:37,967 --> 01:24:39,337
(Leaf mustard kimchi, seasoned dead man's fingers,)

2114
01:24:39,337 --> 01:24:40,467
(bitter lettuce kimchi, pickled fatsia shoots, hairtail stew)

2115
01:24:40,467 --> 01:24:43,037
(Dinner by Jeong Oe Sim)

2116
01:24:44,107 --> 01:24:47,507
(What will the siblings have on their dinner table?)

2117
01:24:48,007 --> 01:24:51,207
We're going to braise the silver pomfret we caught today.

2118
01:24:51,577 --> 01:24:53,877
We're also going to blanch some octopus for parboiled octopus.

2119
01:24:54,077 --> 01:24:56,987
What was it again? Gizzard shad that the others caught yesterday.

2120
01:24:57,317 --> 01:24:58,757
We'll be grilling those as well.

2121
01:24:59,257 --> 01:25:00,417
What about onions? Don't we need some?

2122
01:25:00,417 --> 01:25:02,927
Onions are a must. And green onions.

2123
01:25:03,627 --> 01:25:06,297
Wash them up, and then cut them.

2124
01:25:07,927 --> 01:25:09,327
I'll wash a little of each.

2125
01:25:09,627 --> 01:25:12,037
- Yes, we don't need much of each.
- Okay.

2126
01:25:17,037 --> 01:25:19,577
(Human wasp mode is on.)

2127
01:25:20,307 --> 01:25:22,877
Potatoes are tasty when paired with braised large silver pomfret.

2128
01:25:23,477 --> 01:25:25,047
Braised fish dishes are great...

2129
01:25:25,047 --> 01:25:26,617
when there are lots of potatoes in them.

2130
01:25:26,617 --> 01:25:28,717
(Fluffy potatoes are a must in braised fish.)

2131
01:25:29,587 --> 01:25:34,017
(In the meantime, Chef Yum prepares to cook the parboiled octopus.)

2132
01:25:34,787 --> 01:25:38,027
(Filling the pot with water to barboil the octopus)

2133
01:25:39,727 --> 01:25:42,427
(Did she break the green onion?)

2134
01:25:42,967 --> 01:25:45,797
I'm going to make the broth to parboil the octopus first.

2135
01:25:45,797 --> 01:25:47,837
(Her plan is to make the broth first to parboil the octopus.)

2136
01:25:48,497 --> 01:25:49,767
I'll add green onions, radish,

2137
01:25:51,267 --> 01:25:52,637
sea salt, and soju.

2138
01:25:55,077 --> 01:25:56,607
(This is the secret to removing the fishy smell...)

2139
01:25:56,607 --> 01:25:59,017
(and making the octopus tender.)

2140
01:25:59,647 --> 01:26:01,577
(Flopping down)

2141
01:26:01,577 --> 01:26:05,857
(She puts a lot of effort into parboiling the octopus.)

2142
01:26:06,687 --> 01:26:09,657
(While the broth for the octopus is getting ready,)

2143
01:26:10,687 --> 01:26:13,627
(she starts cleaning the octopus.)

2144
01:26:14,557 --> 01:26:16,767
(It's the one they caught themselves today.)

2145
01:26:17,397 --> 01:26:20,867
(Yeosu's pride, octopus)

2146
01:26:24,307 --> 01:26:27,877
(She cleans it thoroughly before parboiling it.)

2147
01:26:28,607 --> 01:26:29,947
When prepping the octopus,

2148
01:26:29,947 --> 01:26:31,707
do you need to remove feces from the eyes and all?

2149
01:26:31,707 --> 01:26:33,477
- Here. Inside its head is...
- I have no idea.

2150
01:26:33,477 --> 01:26:34,677
Things like octopus ink.

2151
01:26:34,677 --> 01:26:36,747
- The ink is gone already.
- Okay.

2152
01:26:37,247 --> 01:26:38,687
(Cut off the innards...)

2153
01:26:38,687 --> 01:26:40,287
(which create the fishy smell.)

2154
01:26:40,787 --> 01:26:42,527
(Remove the eyes.)

2155
01:26:43,157 --> 01:26:46,857
(And carefully cut off the beak.)

2156
01:26:48,867 --> 01:26:51,627
(Then it's time for a coarse salt...)

2157
01:26:51,627 --> 01:26:54,297
(and flour treatment.)

2158
01:26:54,467 --> 01:26:57,337
I'm removing the debris in the suckers.

2159
01:26:57,337 --> 01:26:58,937
(This process is to remove all kinds of debris,)

2160
01:26:58,937 --> 01:27:00,507
(just like doing laundry.)

2161
01:27:03,207 --> 01:27:06,817
(Lastly, rinse it under clean water several times.)

2162
01:27:09,647 --> 01:27:10,887
It's clean.

2163
01:27:13,087 --> 01:27:16,727
(It took a clean shower.)

2164
01:27:17,427 --> 01:27:21,697
(Meanwhile, the broth has come to a boil.)

2165
01:27:22,167 --> 01:27:25,167
(Finally, it's time to boil the octopus.)

2166
01:27:28,137 --> 01:27:31,767
(She grabs the wild octopus they caught.)

2167
01:27:33,207 --> 01:27:36,107
(Dipping)

2168
01:27:36,807 --> 01:27:39,517
(After dipping the tentacles in and out repeatedly,)

2169
01:27:40,717 --> 01:27:44,947
(the tentacles curl up like a flower.)

2170
01:27:45,687 --> 01:27:47,417
The octopus looks delicious.

2171
01:27:47,417 --> 01:27:49,327
We're having a seafood fiesta today.

2172
01:27:49,327 --> 01:27:50,827
(We're having a seafood fiesta today.)

2173
01:27:51,457 --> 01:27:54,857
(When the octopus takes a shape,)

2174
01:27:56,197 --> 01:27:57,897
I need to set the time.

2175
01:27:58,627 --> 01:28:01,667
(she parboils it for another 10 minutes as it's raw.)

2176
01:28:02,137 --> 01:28:04,837
(Next dish: braised large silver pomfret)

2177
01:28:05,337 --> 01:28:06,507
Hold on. This is...

2178
01:28:06,507 --> 01:28:09,247
(Hold on. This is...)

2179
01:28:09,377 --> 01:28:11,447
They gave us the biggest one.

2180
01:28:12,007 --> 01:28:13,417
They gave us a really big one.

2181
01:28:13,477 --> 01:28:15,787
- Right? There were only small ones.
- Yes.

2182
01:28:16,317 --> 01:28:18,147
They gave us a really good one. Goodness.

2183
01:28:18,647 --> 01:28:20,487
- Gosh. They prep it too.
- They cleaned it for us.

2184
01:28:20,487 --> 01:28:21,657
We just need to rinse it.

2185
01:28:21,657 --> 01:28:22,957
(The large silver pomfret that's shiny, gigantic, and cleaned)

2186
01:28:24,057 --> 01:28:25,497
Okay, large silver pomfret, let's go.

2187
01:28:27,897 --> 01:28:29,527
It's the large silver pomfret, freshly caught.

2188
01:28:29,527 --> 01:28:32,737
(They're having the one fresh out of the sea.)

2189
01:28:34,237 --> 01:28:37,537
(It still looks massive.)

2190
01:28:38,507 --> 01:28:40,277
(She gives a bath to the precious large silver pomfret.)

2191
01:28:40,277 --> 01:28:41,337
Goodness.

2192
01:28:43,747 --> 01:28:44,847
Gosh. There are a lot.

2193
01:28:45,447 --> 01:28:47,577
Are you ready to have some gizzard shad?

2194
01:28:48,017 --> 01:28:49,687
We caught them yesterday.

2195
01:28:50,687 --> 01:28:53,957
(We caught all of these gizzard shad and large silver pomfret.)

2196
01:28:54,287 --> 01:28:57,327
Sun Young, pour in just enough water to cover the radish.

2197
01:28:57,327 --> 01:28:59,427
- Okay.
- And add the soy sauce for soup.

2198
01:28:59,427 --> 01:29:00,497
One tablespoon.

2199
01:29:01,197 --> 01:29:02,267
Two tablespoons.

2200
01:29:02,327 --> 01:29:03,867
When it simmers,

2201
01:29:03,867 --> 01:29:05,327
add some potatoes and boil them together.

2202
01:29:05,327 --> 01:29:08,467
(Adding potatoes, a must for a braised fish)

2203
01:29:09,207 --> 01:29:11,137
Don't turn up the heat too high.

2204
01:29:11,207 --> 01:29:12,637
The heat shouldn't be too high.

2205
01:29:12,637 --> 01:29:14,107
- Cook it over medium heat.
- Medium heat.

2206
01:29:19,277 --> 01:29:22,047
(She starts making the sauce to cover the fish.)

2207
01:29:22,487 --> 01:29:24,287
(Doenjang)

2208
01:29:24,917 --> 01:29:26,817
(Minced garlic)

2209
01:29:26,817 --> 01:29:28,257
Ginger cooking wine.

2210
01:29:28,257 --> 01:29:31,187
(Ginger cooking wine, regular cooking wine)

2211
01:29:31,627 --> 01:29:32,897
I should add a dash of soju.

2212
01:29:32,897 --> 01:29:34,297
(And the taste of Korea)

2213
01:29:34,797 --> 01:29:37,967
(A cup of soju to remove the fishy smell)

2214
01:29:40,967 --> 01:29:44,267
(She adjusts its saltiness with dark soy sauce.)

2215
01:29:45,637 --> 01:29:47,607
(As for the chili powder,)

2216
01:29:47,607 --> 01:29:50,047
(she adds plenty of it...)

2217
01:29:50,047 --> 01:29:52,947
(until the seasoning thickens.)

2218
01:29:54,647 --> 01:29:58,587
(A red hot seasoning, as hot as this summer, is ready.)

2219
01:30:04,027 --> 01:30:05,387
I think Sun Young will love it.

2220
01:30:06,797 --> 01:30:10,927
(The flavor Sun Young loves is the sweet and spicy flavor.)

2221
01:30:11,797 --> 01:30:15,467
(Next on her to-do list is...)

2222
01:30:16,207 --> 01:30:20,537
(scoring the large silver pomfret to help it to absorb the seasoning.)

2223
01:30:21,277 --> 01:30:25,747
(Large silver pomfret tastes like butter.)

2224
01:30:26,317 --> 01:30:28,717
(On the carpet of well-cooked radish, potatoes, onions...)

2225
01:30:28,717 --> 01:30:32,417
(enters the fish.)

2226
01:30:32,917 --> 01:30:35,587
(And here's the climax.)

2227
01:30:36,527 --> 01:30:39,697
(Covering it with the seasoning)

2228
01:30:42,367 --> 01:30:44,497
(She can't help but smile.)

2229
01:30:44,497 --> 01:30:45,497
It looks good.

2230
01:30:45,797 --> 01:30:47,337
It looks good.

2231
01:30:48,537 --> 01:30:52,707
(After covering the fish with the spicy and sweet sauce,)

2232
01:30:53,237 --> 01:30:57,377
(simmer it over medium heat until it absorbs the sauce well.)

2233
01:30:58,007 --> 01:31:00,647
Is there anything to keep in mind when grilling the gizzard shad?

2234
01:31:01,517 --> 01:31:03,147
I've never grilled them before.

2235
01:31:03,147 --> 01:31:05,957
(Sun Young starts to grill the gizzard shad.)

2236
01:31:06,857 --> 01:31:08,927
Do I grill them in the cooking oil?

2237
01:31:09,327 --> 01:31:11,227
Do you plan to fry them in the cooking oil?

2238
01:31:11,357 --> 01:31:12,657
- That'll work. Yes.
- I have no idea.

2239
01:31:12,727 --> 01:31:13,927
You should do that when you're using a pan.

2240
01:31:13,927 --> 01:31:15,227
I've never grilled gizzard shad before either.

2241
01:31:15,297 --> 01:31:18,497
(She thoroughly pats the fish dry.)

2242
01:31:19,067 --> 01:31:21,537
Let's see how much oil splatters.

2243
01:31:21,537 --> 01:31:23,607
(Securing a safe distance)

2244
01:31:27,037 --> 01:31:29,507
(Splattering)

2245
01:31:30,407 --> 01:31:31,407
(Scared)

2246
01:31:31,407 --> 01:31:32,577
Gosh. It splatters quite a bit.

2247
01:31:34,317 --> 01:31:35,417
(Popping)

2248
01:31:37,087 --> 01:31:40,017
(She evacuates in case the fish explodes.)

2249
01:31:44,957 --> 01:31:48,227
(The gizzard shad are getting popped, not grilled.)

2250
01:31:48,527 --> 01:31:49,667
It's getting crazy.

2251
01:31:51,927 --> 01:31:53,137
I patted them dry, though.

2252
01:31:54,197 --> 01:31:57,067
(She stands from a distance...)

2253
01:31:57,067 --> 01:31:58,737
(and drops the fish.)

2254
01:31:59,407 --> 01:32:01,277
(As there's plenty of oil,)

2255
01:32:01,277 --> 01:32:04,177
(the gizzard shad are getting nicely cooked,)

2256
01:32:04,177 --> 01:32:06,917
(though the oil splatters a bit.)

2257
01:32:07,447 --> 01:32:09,617
We cook the gizzard shad we caught.

2258
01:32:10,017 --> 01:32:12,217
We cook the large silver pomfret we caught.

2259
01:32:12,657 --> 01:32:15,587
It's an incredible thing that we're cooking what we caught.

2260
01:32:15,587 --> 01:32:18,027
- Jung Ah. Gosh. It's impossible.
- No one...

2261
01:32:18,027 --> 01:32:19,357
can experience something like this.

2262
01:32:19,357 --> 01:32:22,027
(Grilling the fish after they caught them)

2263
01:32:23,667 --> 01:32:27,267
(Literally, they're fresh off the sea.)

2264
01:32:29,437 --> 01:32:30,667
Hello, viewers.

2265
01:32:31,837 --> 01:32:32,907
These are gizzard shad.

2266
01:32:33,037 --> 01:32:35,137
You watched me catch them, right?

2267
01:32:35,477 --> 01:32:36,547
These precious gizzard shad.

2268
01:32:36,707 --> 01:32:38,347
I'm grilling them for you.

2269
01:32:38,877 --> 01:32:40,917
But we'll be the ones eating them on your behalf.

2270
01:32:43,887 --> 01:32:46,487
(I'll grill them nicely for you.)

2271
01:32:46,487 --> 01:32:47,887
(From Sun Young)

2272
01:32:49,127 --> 01:32:51,157
I'll stop parboiling the octopus.

2273
01:32:52,957 --> 01:32:55,657
(The beautifully curled octopus looks tasty.)

2274
01:32:56,227 --> 01:32:58,697
(Is it cooked well?)

2275
01:32:58,697 --> 01:32:59,867
I'll cool it down.

2276
01:33:01,137 --> 01:33:05,267
(Bouncy)

2277
01:33:06,077 --> 01:33:07,507
You need to cool down.

2278
01:33:08,477 --> 01:33:10,307
Stay in front of the fan.

2279
01:33:10,307 --> 01:33:12,077
(Enjoying the breeze from the fan)

2280
01:33:12,717 --> 01:33:15,247
We caught the octopus ourselves today.

2281
01:33:15,247 --> 01:33:18,317
(The octopus looks chewier because they caught it today.)

2282
01:33:19,017 --> 01:33:22,757
(Perfect)

2283
01:33:24,257 --> 01:33:26,197
(Meanwhile...)

2284
01:33:26,197 --> 01:33:27,857
I get to grill gizzard shad...

2285
01:33:28,097 --> 01:33:30,927
I caught and pulled out of the net myself.

2286
01:33:33,337 --> 01:33:36,437
(The grilled gizzard shad are looking perfect too.)

2287
01:33:36,807 --> 01:33:37,807
(Barking)

2288
01:33:38,167 --> 01:33:39,177
(Barking)

2289
01:33:39,637 --> 01:33:42,307
I know. You want some too. They're barking now.

2290
01:33:42,307 --> 01:33:45,447
They want to have some after smelling them.

2291
01:33:46,117 --> 01:33:47,377
(Grilled gizzard shad!)

2292
01:33:47,377 --> 01:33:48,377
(Give them!)

2293
01:33:48,617 --> 01:33:49,917
The daughter-in-law who ran away...

